    <p class="ai-model-kicker">Frontier models</p>
    <h1 class="ai-model-title">Gemini 4 Argon</h1>
    <p class="ai-model-dek">Gemini 4 Argon is a Google DeepMind model announced 30 September 2026 and generally available, meaning anyone who pays can use it, since 13 October 2026. Before that, Google offered it to trusted cyber defenders in the <a href="https://deepmind.google/fairwind-program/">Fairwind Program</a>, plus its own teams and some early-access companies and researchers. In the Gemini app and AI Mode in Search, it is for Google AI Pro and Ultra subscribers only. The API id, the name a program uses to call the model, is <code>gemini-4-argon</code>. The introductory price is $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens. Tokens are the word pieces models are billed by. Cached input, text the service has already seen and stored, is 95% off. Google says the intro price ends 30 November 2026, and then $4 and $20 apply.</p>
    <hr class="ai-model-rule" />
    <ModelFacts />
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Training</h2>
    <p>Google says it trained Argon for cybersecurity defense, and that prompt-injection robustness included adversarial training. Google says it monitored training runs, sent alerts to an incident-response team, and did not feed those findings back into training so the model would not learn to hide from the monitor. Parameter count, training compute, dataset, and knowledge cutoff are not in the 30 September post. Google’s 13 October post links a Gemini 4 Argon model card.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Inference</h2>
    <p>Google does not publish tokens per second, rate limits, regions, or thinking-level controls in the announce. Artificial Analysis lists speed as unknown for Gemini 4 Argon (High), and says that page is the reasoning version. Google says the model is multimodal. Google does not publish an input and output modality list.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Context</h2>
    <p>Context is how much text the model can take in and write in one job. Google’s 13 October post describes a “1M unified input-output token limit via the Gemini API,” meaning 1 million tokens shared between what goes in and what comes out. The 30 September post said the output token limit is 1 million tokens, up from 64,000. Artificial Analysis lists a 1 million token context window for Gemini 4 Argon (High). That figure is Artificial Analysis’s, not a second Google sentence.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Methods</h2>
    <p>For trusted defenders and Google’s own teams, Google says it is releasing Argon without cyber guardrails. The broader model is designed to refuse harmful cyber and chemical, biological, radiological, and nuclear requests, under Google’s Frontier Safety Framework. Google says it monitors internal activations for misuse, and that misalignment checks watch the chain of thought and the actions and can stop execution. Google says Argon is its most resilient model yet against indirect prompt injection. Indirect prompt injection means hidden instructions in a webpage, email, or file that try to hijack the model. Google’s 13 October post says Argon leads Gray Swan’s Indirect Prompt Injection benchmark, with attack success cut to 0.7% across 15 adaptive attempts. That is Google’s figure. The API id <code>gemini-4-argon</code> now exists. The 30 September post did not name public tools.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Benchmarking</h2>
    <p>Artificial Analysis scores Gemini 4 Argon (High) at 50 on its Intelligence Index v5.0, fetched 28 October 2026 (53 on the older v4.3.2 index, fetched 1 October 2026). Artificial Analysis moved to Intelligence Index v5.0 on 27 October 2026, a new nine-test set on a new scale, so v5.0 scores do not line up with older v4.3.2 numbers. That is an independent score, not a Google chart number.</p>
    <p>Google’s newer <a href="/ai/models/google/gemini-4-carbon/">Gemini 4 Carbon</a>, announced 28 October 2026, scores 59 on the same index. Google says Carbon builds on Argon. It does not say Carbon replaces Argon.</p>
    <p>The cells below are Google’s 30 September post. They are lab claims.</p>
